WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:08.480
Here is an incredible paper that teaches virtual&nbsp;
humans to run in the most fabulous ways. So good!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:16.040
So, what is this? How did we get here? These are&nbsp;
large language models, text-based AI systems that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:23.120
can be our smart assistant, someone who can&nbsp;
draw images for us, we all know that. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:29.400
fewer Fellow Scholars know that they are also&nbsp;
excellent at reading and writing computer code,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:37.720
and can thus, even learn to play Minecraft. That&nbsp;
is incredible. Just think about it: this is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:45.080
text-based AI, how could it possibly control&nbsp;
a graphical game like this? Really amazing.

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:49.040
So, at this point, we know what&nbsp;
these large language models can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:56.480
do. The age of surprises is over…or&nbsp;
so I thought! Dear Fellow Scholars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.480 --> 00:00:59.880
this is Two Minute Papers&nbsp;
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:03.640
Previous AI-based techniques can play Atari games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:10.160
and other games at a superhuman level. They&nbsp;
typically do it through reinforcement learning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:17.520
which means that they get a controller, look at&nbsp;
the screen and play like a human would. DeepMind’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:24.560
techniques are excellent at this, and thus,&nbsp;
they already do extremely well on these games.

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:34.080
However, there is a huge problem. What is the&nbsp;
problem? Well, look. Scores. Games have scores.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:42.000
And these scores can be used as feedback for the&nbsp;
AI to understand whether it is doing well or not.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:50.040
High score, doing well, lower score, not so much.&nbsp;
And whatever other task we wish it to perform,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:57.440
there needs to be a score. There needs to be&nbsp;
feedback. And therein lies the problem. Each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:03.480
game has a different scoring mechanism,&nbsp;
and thus, each task needs a different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:10.120
scoring mechanism. If we are aiming to create an&nbsp;
intelligence of sorts, any kind of intelligence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:18.920
requires generality. So it has to be able to&nbsp;
perform tasks it hasn’t seen before. But how?

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:26.720
Scientists at NVIDIA had a crazy idea. Let’s&nbsp;
use these ChatGPT-ish large language models&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:35.240
to write code to calculate the score for&nbsp;
these tasks by itself. Wow. I love it. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:39.040
does it work? I think that cannot possibly work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:45.560
there are just too many kinds of tasks out&nbsp;
there. Well, let’s have a look together.

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:51.840
Now, little AI, write code for the score&nbsp;
for this humanoid to run and then, train&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:58.600
it. Well, what can I say… it works. Well,&nbsp;
look, no one said it has to be beautiful.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:04.840
We said it has to run. Running is making&nbsp;
progress moving forward. It recognized that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:10.560
and this already works, and the&nbsp;
whole thing was written by an AI.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:15.760
Now, get this. Let’s try this&nbsp;
some other way. First, little AI,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:23.720
design a task where forward movement is&nbsp;
necessary. Wow. That is a fabulous way of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:30.360
moving forward. Now let’s give it some feedback:&nbsp;
This looks like squat jumps, make the movement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:39.880
resemble running a little more. So the AI says,&nbsp;
okay, got it, so you mean a duck walk! Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:48.080
not quite! New feedback: the torso has&nbsp;
to be a little higher. Okay, good. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:57.920
it is using mostly one leg to hop on. Please use&nbsp;
both legs. Goodness! Both legs are now being used,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:06.120
but look. Hoo boy! Back to square one. Now the&nbsp;
torso is too low again, let’s ask it to penalize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:14.160
that, and…there we go. Finally, something that&nbsp;
resembles running. It is not an easy task at all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:21.440
it turns out. And, this human feedback-driven&nbsp;
solution I think is way better than the AI on its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:28.000
own. This intelligence of sorts is wonderful,&nbsp;
but human intelligence is where it’s at.

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:35.920
What a fantastic paper! But, it gets better.&nbsp;
In fact, here comes the best part! This concept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:43.680
generalizes to so many tasks, from passing balls&nbsp;
to balancing them, teaching robots to move,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:51.480
and even the crows favorite: spinning pens. Very&nbsp;
impressive. But, how does this train so well and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:59.800
so quickly? The key is that this learns within a&nbsp;
computer simulation which has two advantages: one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:07.960
it can’t really harm itself, and two, my favorite:&nbsp;
in the real world, one second means one second,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:15.000
but in a virtual world, one real second can be&nbsp;
simulated much quicker, given a powerful computer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:22.320
A little game can be simulated quicker. How much&nbsp;
quicker? Well, in this case, this little AI is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:29.600
learning a thousand times quicker in a simulation&nbsp;
than it would in the real world. So cool!

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:33.880
And it gets even better. Hold on&nbsp;
to your papers Fellow Scholars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:39.080
because it can not only match the&nbsp;
level of humans playing these games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:46.200
but the evolution-based variant can even&nbsp;
showcase superhuman performance. Wow! It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:55.400
matches or exceeds the level of humans on 75%&nbsp;
of the dexterity-based tasks. That is insanity.

00:05:55.400 --> 00:06:01.920
Finally, an AI that can do these tasks and&nbsp;
have some sort of generality. What we are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:09.160
seeing here is perhaps the early days of true&nbsp;
intelligence being born. And all this within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:17.120
a little piece of silicon, and lots and lots of&nbsp;
human ingenuity. What a time to be alive! However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:23.160
it is a research work, and it is not&nbsp;
perfect. Not even close. For instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:27.640
please don’t ask it to close your doors. Ouch.

